{
  "script": [
    {
      "beatId": "b1",
      "narration": "In March 2025, payment company Larkspur Pay lost thirty-one percent of its checkouts. The failure lasted forty-seven minutes. Its rate limiter's store went slow.",
      "headline": {
        "text": "Slow limiter store broke checkout",
        "semantic": "problem"
      },
      "tags": [
        "Larkspur Pay",
        "store slow"
      ],
      "statCards": [
        {
          "value": "31%",
          "label": "checkouts failed",
          "sourceRef": "num-19"
        }
      ],
      "source": "Larkspur Pay platform team, 14 March 2025"
    },
    {
      "beatId": "b2",
      "narration": "A rate limiter counts each caller's requests and refuses the extras. Each caller has a token bucket: one token buys one checkout. It refills ten a second, capped at a hundred.",
      "headline": {
        "text": "Token bucket = permission tokens",
        "semantic": "concept"
      },
      "tags": [
        "refill rate",
        "capacity"
      ]
    },
    {
      "beatId": "b3",
      "narration": "The gateway runs twenty-four nodes. Per-node buckets would give a customer twenty-four times its limit. So the bucket lives in one shared store, one place every node reads.",
      "headline": {
        "text": "Per-node buckets multiply the limit",
        "semantic": "problem"
      },
      "tags": [
        "per-node buckets",
        "one shared count"
      ]
    },
    {
      "beatId": "b4",
      "narration": "Now two nodes check at once. Without an atomic script, one indivisible read-refill-write, both take the last token. With it, the loser gets a four-two-nine refusal.",
      "headline": {
        "text": "Atomic script = one indivisible step",
        "semantic": "concept"
      },
      "tags": [
        "last token",
        "read-refill-write"
      ]
    },
    {
      "beatId": "b5",
      "narration": "But that store adds four tenths of a millisecond to every request, at the median. Past fifty milliseconds, a store call counts as failed, and the policy flips.",
      "headline": {
        "text": "Slow store flips the policy",
        "semantic": "problem"
      },
      "tags": [
        "store timeout",
        "policy flip"
      ],
      "statCards": [
        {
          "value": "50 ms",
          "label": "store timeout",
          "sourceRef": "num-16"
        }
      ],
      "source": "quotaflow"
    },
    {
      "beatId": "b6",
      "narration": "Then a failover made store calls jump to eighty milliseconds. Checkout failed open, serving traffic it could not count. The provider got forty times its contracted rate and throttled every checkout.",
      "headline": {
        "text": "Fail open multiplies the limit",
        "semantic": "problem"
      },
      "tags": [
        "fail open",
        "store failover"
      ],
      "statCards": [
        {
          "value": "40 times",
          "label": "the contracted rate",
          "sourceRef": "num-23"
        }
      ],
      "source": "Larkspur Pay platform team, 14 March 2025"
    },
    {
      "beatId": "b7",
      "narration": "So checkout fails closed: it refuses requests rather than serve traffic it cannot count. Fallback limits are split by instance count, and the store timeout rose to a hundred fifty milliseconds.",
      "headline": {
        "text": "Fail closed under slow store",
        "semantic": "resolution"
      },
      "tags": [
        "fail closed",
        "split fallback"
      ],
      "statCards": [
        {
          "value": "150 ms",
          "label": "new store timeout",
          "sourceRef": "num-25"
        }
      ],
      "source": "Post-incident report: checkout errors on 14 March 2025"
    }
  ]
}

